{%- comment -%}
  "Have a discount code?" — collapsed, in the cart.

  Usage: {% render 'cart-coupon', uid: 'drawer' %}

  Shown but closed, which is the whole point. A code box sitting open with a
  blank field is an advertisement that a discount exists somewhere, and the
  shopper leaves to go and find it — often onto a coupon site, sometimes
  back through someone else's affiliate link, frequently not back at all.
  Absent, and anyone actually holding a code has to carry it to checkout on
  faith. A disclosure is the answer to both: invisible unless you are
  looking for it, and one tap away if you are.

  ── How a code is applied ────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Through /discount/CODE?redirect=…, the same documented route the welcome
  offer uses — see snippets/welcome-offer.liquid, which explains why it is a
  real navigation rather than a background fetch. Shopify attaches the code
  to the cart and hands the shopper straight back.

  This is therefore JavaScript-only, because the code is a path segment and
  a plain GET form cannot build one. The noscript note below says so and
  points at checkout, rather than rendering a field that silently does
  nothing.

  ── Telling the shopper it failed ────────────────────────────────────────

  /discount/ with a bad code redirects back exactly as a good one does,
  having applied nothing and said nothing — so a typo looks identical to
  success. The redirect therefore carries the attempted code back in
  ?coupon_tried=, and app.js compares it against data-applied below, which
  lists what Shopify actually honoured. Match: confirmed. No match: the code
  was not accepted, said plainly.

  Liquid cannot read a query parameter, which is why that comparison is in
  JavaScript and the evidence it needs is published as an attribute here.

  Params
    uid  a suffix for the input id, so the drawer and the cart page can both
         render this without colliding. Labels need an id to point at.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign cc_uid = uid | default: 'cart'

  # What Shopify is actually honouring, upcased, so app.js can compare
  # against the attempted code without caring how the merchant typed it.
  assign cc_applied = ''
  for cc_d in cart.cart_level_discount_applications
    assign cc_title = cc_d.title | upcase
    assign cc_applied = cc_applied | append: cc_title | append: ','
  endfor

  # Come back to this page, not the home page. request.path drops the query
  # string deliberately: nothing here needs filters or pagination carried.
  assign cc_back = request.path | default: '/'
-%}

<div class="ccoupon" data-coupon data-applied="{{ cc_applied | escape }}" data-back="{{ cc_back | escape }}">
  <details class="ccoupon-fold">
    <summary class="ccoupon-summary">
      <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">local_offer</span>
      <span>Have a discount code?</span>
      <span class="mi ccoupon-chev" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
    </summary>

    <div class="ccoupon-body">
      <label class="visually-hidden" for="coupon-{{ cc_uid }}">Discount code</label>
      <div class="ccoupon-row">
        {%- comment -%}
          type="text" with autocapitalize on: codes are conventionally
          upper-case and a phone keyboard that lower-cases them makes the
          field look broken. Shopify matches case-insensitively, so this is
          presentation — it just needs to look like what is on the email.

          Not type="search" and not a form: Enter is handled in app.js, and
          a bare <form> here would nest inside the cart page's checkout form.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <input class="ccoupon-input"
               id="coupon-{{ cc_uid }}"
               type="text"
               name="coupon"
               autocomplete="off"
               autocapitalize="characters"
               spellcheck="false"
               placeholder="Enter code"
               data-coupon-input
               aria-describedby="coupon-msg-{{ cc_uid }}">
        <button type="button" class="ccoupon-apply" data-coupon-apply>Apply</button>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        One live region for both outcomes, so a screen reader announces the
        result of applying a code without the field losing focus.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="ccoupon-msg" id="coupon-msg-{{ cc_uid }}" data-coupon-msg role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>

      <noscript>
        <p class="ccoupon-msg is-note">Enter your code at checkout — the field there applies it to this order.</p>
      </noscript>
    </div>
  </details>
</div>
